    "result_text": "Owners now get `membershipTier` `GOLD` when their household has 3 or more members after they're created. The full test suite passes (5 tests, 0 failures), including the new acceptance test `coreGoldForThreeMemberHousehold`.\n\n**How it works:** existing values like `namesakeCount` are fixed when the owner is created, so I did the same for household size.\n- **New field:** a nullable `household_size INTEGER` column on `owners`, added to all four schema files (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres), plus `Owner.householdSize`.\n- **Counting:** when a new owner is saved, `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner` sets the size to the number of owners already sharing their `householdId`, plus one. Owners with no household get 1. This uses a new `OwnerRepository.countByHouseholdId`, implemented for JDBC, JPA and Spring Data JPA.\n- **Tier:** `OwnerMapper.toMembershipTier` returns `GOLD` when the size is 3 or more; otherwise the existing SILVER/BRONZE rules apply unchanged.\n- **Docs:** I updated the `membershipTier` description in `openapi.yml` to match.\n\n**Behaviours to be aware of:**\n- **Earlier members aren't upgraded.** When the third member joins, only that owner becomes GOLD; the first two keep their original tier. That's how I read \"after this create\". If you want the whole household upgraded, I can compute it when the owner is read instead.\n- **Existing rows stay as they are.** Owners created before this change have no size stored, so none of them become GOLD. There's no migration step to fill it in.\n- **The JDBC version is untested.** The tests only use the Spring Data JPA profile, so the JDBC changes compile but haven't been run.",
